Anna —

The road is ours and the field army is finished. The hired slingers ran the moment the silver stopped looking likely — mercenaries keep excellent accounts. The knights who knelt for pay did not run, and I will give them that much in whatever book gets written.

We reached the walls of Castle Corven at dusk, and then the strangest thing in this whole strange war happened: the gates opened from the inside.

The castle folk had heard about Harrowfield. The Duke's own porters, his grooms, his kitchen — they had all done the same arithmetic: a man who hires goblins against manors will hire worse against cities. A cook named Tam drew the bar off the postern gate himself and said, "He's in the throne hall. He's been sitting up there for days."

So there will be no siege. There will be a walk through a cold keep, past guards who must now decide — each one alone, at the moment we reach them — what exactly the Winter Duke is still paying them for.

And at the end of it, a hall, and a chair, and a man in mourning grey who murdered a king on his own chapel stairs.

Aldric says the war does not end when the Duke dies. It ends when someone lawful sits in that chair, even for a minute, in front of witnesses. So that is the plan, the whole plan, the same plan as the fords: we do not need to kill them all.

We need the throne.

— Elin
